<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orissa Tributary States</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orissa Tributary Mahals</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> Tributary Mahals were the group of small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> in the hills of Orissa, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, that paid tribute to the British after the conquest of 1803. They were supervised by a British superintendent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a> and from 1933 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-states-agency">Eastern States Agency</a>. In 1948 almost all of them merged into the Indian province of Orissa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orissa-tributary-states.jpg" alt="Orissa Tributary States" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Great Britain India Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orissa_Tributary_States-IGI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The states, also known as the Garhjats or the Orissa Feudatory States, lay in the Garhjat Hills, the hilly and once heavily forested region of eastern Orissa on the borders of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>. In the 18th century the region came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, in particular the Bhonsle maharajas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>. At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a> in 1803 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Nagpur ceded Orissa to the British, who ruled part of it directly from the Bengal Presidency and left other parts to local rulers bound to them by subsidiary alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The British recognised the local chiefs as tributary chiefs and their estates as the Tributary Mahals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>. The states were managed by the Political Department and had no regular settlement or revenue system. There were nineteen at first, but the British annexed two: Banki in 1840, after its ruler was convicted of murder, and Angul in 1847, after its Raja opposed the officers sent to suppress the Meriah sacrifice among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondh">Khonds</a>. The largest were Mayurbhanj, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kendujhar">Keonjhar</a>, Dhenkanal, Baudh and Nayagarh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Their status stayed unclear until 1888, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a> accepted that they were not part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and the chiefs received modified powers under a superintendent. In 1905 five Oriya-speaking states, Bamra, Rairakhol, Sonpur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> and Kalahandi, were added from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>, and Gangpur and Bonai from the Chota Nagpur States, bringing the total area to 72,638 square kilometres with 3,173,395 people at the 1901 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><p>From 1912 the states came under the governor of the new province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>. They were later placed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-states-agency">Eastern States Agency</a>, under the Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> rather than a provincial governor. After independence in 1947 the rulers acceded to India and formed the Eastern States Union, hoping for a unit large enough to form a state of its own. The union failed, and all except Saraikela and Kharsawan were integrated into Orissa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Orissa Tributary States” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It paid tribute to the Gajapati Kingdom, the Marathas and, from 1803, the British, who grouped it among the <b>Orissa Tributary Mahals</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/athgarh-state">Athgarh State</a></li><li><blockquote>It was linked with the Sambalpur group of chiefships under the Marathas and passed under British supervision in the 19th century, joining the <b>Orissa Tributary States</b> in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/state/athmallik-state">Athmallik State</a></li><li><blockquote>It paid tribute to the Marathas and from 1803 to the British as one of the <b>Orissa Tributary Mahals</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/baramba-state">Baramba State</a></li><li><blockquote>It paid tribute to the Marathas and later belonged to the <b>Orissa Tributary States</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/baudh-state">Baudh State</a></li><li><blockquote>It was counted among the Chota Nagpur tributary states until 1905, when it was transferred to the <b>Orissa Tributary States</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bonai-state">Bonai State</a></li><li><blockquote>It was tributary to the Marathas and, from 1803, one of the <b>Orissa Tributary Mahals</b> under British supervision.</blockquote><a href="/state/daspalla-state">Daspalla State</a></li></ul></section>
